#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705618 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- e^{- x + y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{- x + y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})&=\\frac{1}{2 e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|&=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},- \\frac{\\pi}{6})\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} - 1\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705625 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - y} \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Apply both product and chain rules to each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right) e^{x - y} \\\\ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)&=\\frac{e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v \\\\ \\mathbf v&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf v\\|^2&=\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} e^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude; the common exponential factor is positive and cancels:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{3},0)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,j}{2 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705630 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y + 2 x - 2 y^{2} - 2 y + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - y + 2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x - 4 y - 2 \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(1,-1)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-1\\right)^2+\\left(1\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,-1)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,-1)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705631 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y + x + 2 y^{2} + y + 3$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - y + 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x + 4 y + 1 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\left(7\\right)\\,i+\\left(-6\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(-1,-2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(7\\right)^2+\\left(-6\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(-1,-2)}{\\|\\nabla f(-1,-2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(7\\right)\\,i+\\left(-6\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{85}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{6 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705641 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y + x + 2 y^{2} - 2 y + 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=4 x + 2 y + 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 x + 4 y - 2 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,2)&=\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(-1,2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right)^2+\\left(4\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(-1,2)}{\\|\\nabla f(-1,2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(1\\right)\\,i+\\left(4\\right)\\,j}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705643 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x y + \\ln{\\left(x + 2 y \\right)} - 1$ that passes through the point $(x,y)=(5,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates. Here the logarithm has positive argument $1$, so the function is differentiable near the point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=y + \\frac{1}{x + 2 y} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=x + \\frac{2}{x + 2 y} \\\\ \\nabla f(5,-2)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j \\\\ \\|\\nabla f(5,-2)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-1\\right)^2+\\left(7\\right)^2} \\\\ &=5 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf n&=\\frac{\\nabla f(5,-2)}{\\|\\nabla f(5,-2)\\|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\left(-1\\right)\\,i+\\left(7\\right)\\,j}{5 \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{7 \\sqrt{2}}{10}\\right)\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705863 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=x - y - 2, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\mathbf i- \\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork where $x - y - 2 > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)})(1) \\\\ &\\quad+(x - y - 2)\\left(\\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(1) \\\\ &=\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)})(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(x - y - 2)\\left(- \\frac{1}{x - y - 2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(-1) \\\\ &=- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n(\\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} + 1)\\mathbf i+(- \\ln{\\left(x - y - 2 \\right)} - 1)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705867 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y,z)&=x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2}, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\begin{aligned}&(2 x y z + 2 y z^{2})\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+(x^{2} z + 2 x z^{2})\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}+(x^{2} y + 4 x y z)\\mathbf k\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{x y z}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\begin{aligned}&- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\mathbf j \\\\ &{}- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\mathbf k\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssume $x\\ne0$, $y\\ne0$, $z\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(2 x y z + 2 y z^{2}) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(x + z\\right)}{x} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{x}\\right) \\\\ &=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} z + 2 x z^{2}) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{x + 2 z}{y} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{y}\\right) \\\\ &=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{z}&=g f_{z}+f g_{z} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} y + 4 x y z) \\\\ &\\quad+(x^{2} y z + 2 x y z^{2})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{x + 4 z}{z} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x + 2 z}{z}\\right) \\\\ &=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705868 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=x y, \\\\ \\nabla f&=y\\mathbf i+x\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{1}{x}\\mathbf i+\\frac{1}{y}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWork where $2 x y > 0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)})(y) \\\\ &\\quad+(x y)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ &=y \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(y) \\\\ &=y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)})(x) \\\\ &\\quad+(x y)\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\\\ &=x \\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad+(x) \\\\ &=x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&y \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+x \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 1\\right)\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705885 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}, \\\\ \\nabla f&=\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf i- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssume $y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(\\frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{4 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{4 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\\left(- \\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad+(\\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)})\\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 \\left(2 x \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)} + y\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\frac{- 2 x + y \\arctan{\\left(\\frac{2 x}{y} \\right)}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705890 :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=- x^{2} - y, \\\\ \\nabla f&=- 2 x\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\ g(x,y)&=2 x^{2} y^{2}, \\\\ \\nabla g&=4 x y^{2}\\mathbf i+4 x^{2} y\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the **product rule**: $\\nabla(fg)=g\\nabla f+f\\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.\n\nNow collect each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\\\ &=(2 x^{2} y^{2})(- 2 x) \\\\ &\\quad+(- x^{2} - y)(4 x y^{2}) \\\\ &=- 4 x^{3} y^{2} \\\\ &\\quad+(- 4 x y^{2} \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)) \\\\ &=- 4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\\\ &=(2 x^{2} y^{2})(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(- x^{2} - y)(4 x^{2} y) \\\\ &=- 2 x^{2} y^{2} \\\\ &\\quad+(- 4 x^{2} y \\left(x^{2} + y\\right)) \\\\ &=- 2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient is\n\n$$\n- 4 x y^{2} \\left(2 x^{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf i- 2 x^{2} y \\left(2 x^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nCollect the contributions to each coordinate separately."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705927 :question/problem "Find parametric equations of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\ln{\\left(\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2} \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=1$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(x - 1\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(y - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{2 \\left(z - 2\\right)}{\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 2\\right)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(x - 1\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(z - 2\\right)&=e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)}{\\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2} + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2)=\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 0,0,\\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\rangle=\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,2,e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,0,e^{- \\frac{1}{2}}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nEquating coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x&=1\\\\y&=2\\\\z&=\\frac{t}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}} + e^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705939 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&-4 \\left(- x - 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(1 - y\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&=12\\\\\n&{}+\\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-6 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(-1,0,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=-\\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-4 \\left(- x - 1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(1 - y\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+6 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=12$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=8 \\left(- x - 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-8 \\left(1 - y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=6\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(z + 1\\right)&=2\\\\\n\\left(- x - 1\\right)&=0\\\\\n\\left(1 - y\\right)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=8 \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-8 \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=6\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(-1,0,1)=\\langle 0,-8,2\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $2$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=2\\langle 0,-8,2\\rangle=\\langle 0,-16,4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle -1,0,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 0,-16,4\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705940 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-\\left(- x - 2\\right)\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(- x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=0$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(- x - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=-4 \\left(z + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- x - 2\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n\\left(z + 1\\right)&=1\\\\\n\\left(y - 2\\right)&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=1\\\\\n&{}+4 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=1\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=-4 \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(- \\frac{5}{2},5,0)=\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nMultiply every component by the same nonzero scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ to obtain the equivalent direction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2}\\langle 3,1,-4\\rangle=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle - \\frac{5}{2},5,0\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},-2\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705950 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} \\left(- z\\right)\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- z\\right)^{2} \\left(- y - 2\\right)\\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(3,-4,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} \\left(- z\\right)\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- z\\right)^{2} \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=5$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- z\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\\left(- z\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=-\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(- z\\right) \\left(- y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x - 2\\right)&=1\\\\\n\\left(- z\\right)&=-1\\\\\n\\left(- y - 2\\right)&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}+2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-\\left(-1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=-\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\\\\n&{}-2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(3,-4,1)=\\langle 2,-5,3\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 3,-4,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle 2,-5,3\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705956 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nat $P(0,-3,1)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms together and set\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y - 2\\right) x z\\\\\n&{}+\\ln{\\left(\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others fixed. Use the product and chain rules, including signs from inner linear functions:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(x,y,z)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}-x z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(- y - 2\\right) + x + z}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(- y - 2\\right) x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $P$, the linear expressions in these formulas have the values\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(- y - 2\\right)&=1\\\\\nx&=0\\\\\nz&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute these values into the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{x}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{y}(P)&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}-\\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\small\\begin{aligned}\nF_{z}(P)&=\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(1\\right)}\\\\\n&{}+\\left(1\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $\\nabla F(0,-3,1)=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf 0$. A normal line passes through $P$ and has this direction.\n\nThe point supplies the constant vector, and the normal supplies the direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 0,-3,1\\rangle+t\\,\\langle \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705959 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x^{2} + 4 x \\left(y + 2\\right) - x - 2 y - 4=x + 2 y + 4.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=2 x^{2} + 4 x \\left(y + 2\\right) - 2 x - 4 y - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=0.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=4 x + 4 y + 6 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=4 x - 4 \\\\\n\\nabla f(2,-3)&=\\left\\langle 2,4\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 2,4\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(4\\right)\\left(x - 2\\right)&=\\left(2\\right)\\left(y + 3\\right) \\\\\n4 x - 2 y - 14&=0 \\\\\n2 x - y - 7&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $2 x - y - 7=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705960 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y - 2\\right) - 8=2 x + \\left(x + 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} - 3.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-3+\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}+\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}+2 x \\\\\n&{}- \\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y - 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=-8.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x - y + 8 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- x + 2 y - 6 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-4,1)&=\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle -1,0\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(0\\right)\\left(x + 4\\right)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\left(y - 1\\right) \\\\\ny - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $y - 1=0.$ This is a horizontal line. The direction relation avoids division by a zero gradient component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705969 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&-2+\\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}- 2 y+3 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&=11+4 \\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-2+\\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}- 2 y \\\\\n&{}+3 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}- 4 \\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=11.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=6 x - 4 y + 2 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- 4 x + 2 y - 4 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-2,1)&=\\left\\langle -14,6\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle -14,6\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(6\\right)\\left(x + 2\\right)&=\\left(-14\\right)\\left(y - 1\\right) \\\\\n6 x + 14 y - 2&=0 \\\\\n3 x + 7 y - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $3 x + 7 y - 1=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705980 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6 x \\left(y + 2\\right) + 13=x^{2} - 2 y + 5 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2} - 4.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=-4+x^{2}- 2 y+5 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&{}- 6 x \\left(y + 2\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=13.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2 x - 6 y - 12 \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=- 6 x + 10 y + 18 \\\\\n\\nabla f(-1,-4)&=\\left\\langle 10,-16\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 10,-16\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(-16\\right)\\left(x + 1\\right)&=\\left(10\\right)\\left(y + 4\\right) \\\\\n- 16 x - 10 y - 56&=0 \\\\\n8 x + 5 y + 28&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $8 x + 5 y + 28=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705984 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x + y + \\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 1=2 x + y - 5.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the curve gives the level set $f(x,y)=-4.$ Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other coordinate fixed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=3 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{3} \\left(2 y + 2\\right) \\\\\n\\nabla f(0,-3)&=\\left\\langle 12,4\\right\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it gives a direction for the normal line. A line through the given point parallel to $\\left\\langle 12,4\\right\\rangle$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(x-x_0)&=a(y-y_0) \\\\\n\\left(4\\right)\\left(x\\right)&=\\left(12\\right)\\left(y + 3\\right) \\\\\n4 x - 12 y - 36&=0 \\\\\nx - 3 y - 9&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the whole equation gives the normal line $x - 3 y - 9=0.$ Its direction is parallel to the gradient; using the gradient as the coefficient vector would give the tangent line instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706065 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{\\sin\\theta}2r\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant. Substitute the angular expression for the upper radial limit only after finding the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\left[r^2\\right]_0^{\\sin\\theta}\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^3\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo integrate the odd power, use $\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta$. With $u=\\cos\\theta$ and $\\mathrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta(1-\\cos^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left[-\\cos\\theta+\\frac{\\cos^3\\theta}{3}\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\\\\\n&=0-\\left(-1+\\frac13\\right)=\\frac23.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706068 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_1^2 r^3\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n\\left[\\frac{r^4}{4}\\right]_1^2\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{4}\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, integrate with respect to $\\theta$. Since the derivative of $\\sin^2\\theta$ is $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\frac{15}{8}\\left[\\sin^2\\theta\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{8}(1-0)=\\frac{15}{8}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706075 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\int_{1-\\cos(2\\theta)}^1 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, integrate with respect to $r$, treating $\\theta$ as a constant. The lower radial limit must be squared as a whole:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\frac12\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(1-(1-\\cos(2\\theta))^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(\\cos(2\\theta)-\\frac12\\cos^2(2\\theta)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall $\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1$. Substituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging gives\n\n$$\n\\cos^2(2\\theta)=\\frac{1+\\cos(4\\theta)}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse this identity and integrate with respect to $\\theta$, including the factors from the angular frequencies:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_0^{\\pi/6}\\left(\\cos(2\\theta)-\\frac14-\\frac14\\cos(4\\theta)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d\\theta\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{\\sin(2\\theta)}{2}-\\frac{\\theta}{4}-\\frac{\\sin(4\\theta)}{16}\\right]_0^{\\pi/6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth sine terms survive at the upper endpoint: $\\sin(\\pi/3)=\\sin(2\\pi/3)=\\sqrt3/2$. All three terms vanish at the lower endpoint. Therefore,\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{32}\n=\\frac{7\\sqrt3}{32}-\\frac{\\pi}{24}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706215 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x e^{z} + y e^{x} + z e^{y}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-1,0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y e^{x} + e^{z}\\\\f_y&=z e^{y} + e^{x}\\\\f_z&=x e^{z} + e^{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-1,0,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=\\left(0\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)} + e^{\\left(-1\\right)}=e^{-1}\\\\f_y(P)&=\\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(0\\right)} + e^{\\left(-1\\right)}=-1 + e^{-1}\\\\f_z(P)&=\\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)} + e^{\\left(0\\right)}=1 - e^{-1}\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=e^{-1}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(1 - e^{-1}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla f(P)=\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j+\\left(2 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706216 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^{3} + y^{2} z^{2} + 2 z\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-2,1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=3 x^{2}\\\\f_y&=2 y z^{2}\\\\f_z&=2 y^{2} z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}=12\\\\f_y(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=8\\\\f_z(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) + 2=6\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706221 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,0,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 x\\\\f_y&=4 y\\\\f_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,0,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right)=-4\\\\f_y(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\f_z(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right)=-8\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=-4\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla f(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706223 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(3,-4,0)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\\\\f_y&=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\\\\f_z&=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(3,-4,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=\\frac{\\left(3\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\\\f_y(P)&=\\frac{\\left(-4\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=- \\frac{4}{5}\\\\f_z(P)&=\\frac{\\left(0\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=0\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\mathbf i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706228 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^{3} y z - x y z^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=3 x^{2} y z - y z^{2}\\\\f_y&=x^{3} z - x z^{2}\\\\f_z&=x^{3} y - 2 x y z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,2,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right) - \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=4\\\\f_y(P)&=\\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(2\\right) - \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-2\\\\f_z(P)&=\\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(2\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right)=-6\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706329 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,0,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,0,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) + 4=4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla g(P)=8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706333 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 x^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(0,2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 x^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{3}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(0,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3g^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=3(1)^{2}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=12\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=12\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $12\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706337 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,-1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y\\\\g_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-1\\right)=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(-1\\right)=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla g(P)=8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706340 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-2,-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{3}&=0\\\\2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has cube zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x + 4\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,-2,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(0\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla g(P)=-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706348 :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\n- 2 x^{2} + x z + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}=20 - 2 z\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} + x z + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 z - 20\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 4 x + z\\\\F_y&=4 y\\\\F_z&=x + 4 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,2,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=- 4 \\left(1\\right) + \\left(2\\right)=-2\\\\F_y(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right)=8\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(1\\right) + 4 \\left(2\\right) + 2=11\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=-2\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+11\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706353 :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^{2} - y^{2} + y z - z^{2}=- 2 z - 2\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^{2} - y^{2} + y z - z^{2} + 2 z + 2\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x\\\\F_y&=- 2 y + z\\\\F_z&=y - 2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,0,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)=2\\\\F_y(P)&=- 2 \\left(0\\right) + \\left(-1\\right)=-1\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=4\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla F(P)=-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706355 :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 2 y^{2} - y z + z^{2}=2 z + 13\n$$\n\nat $P=(2,-2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - y z + z^{2} - 2 z - 13\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x\\\\F_y&=4 y - z\\\\F_z&=- y + 2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=2 \\left(2\\right)=4\\\\F_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(-1\\right)=-7\\\\F_z(P)&=- \\left(-2\\right) + 2 \\left(-1\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-7\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(P)=-8\\,\\mathbf i+14\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-8\\,\\mathbf i+14\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706357 :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\n- 2 x^{2} + x z + y^{2} - 2 z^{2}=- z - 10\n$$\n\nat $P=(-2,-2,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} + x z + y^{2} - 2 z^{2} + z + 10\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 4 x + z\\\\F_y&=2 y\\\\F_z&=x - 4 z + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,-2,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=- 4 \\left(-2\\right) + \\left(-2\\right)=6\\\\F_y(P)&=2 \\left(-2\\right)=-4\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(-2\\right) - 4 \\left(-2\\right) + 1=7\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j+7\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla F(P)=-6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706376 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{3}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=2 x - 2\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=4 z - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-2,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=2 \\left(2\\right) - 2=2\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) - 4=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3g^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=3(1)^{2}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=6\\,\\mathbf i-12\\,\\mathbf j-12\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=-12\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-12\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706378 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 3\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 3\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{2}$ and $g(P)=-1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2g^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=2(-1)^{1}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=-8\\,\\mathbf i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=16\\,\\mathbf i.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $16\\,\\mathbf i$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706387 :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{2}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,1,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(0\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2g^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=2(1)^{1}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla f(P)=-8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}]}
